2026\nCatch up on select AI news and developments from the past week: __OpenAI launches always-on dots agents for autonomous workplace tasks.__ OpenAI has introduced dots, always-on agents designed to pursue user goals across applications with limited supervision. Powered by GPT-6 Astra, dots can work through Slack and Teams, manage evolving projects, update sales proposals, research information, analyze data, prepare documents, and build software using Codex and ChatGPT Work. Users can establish rules governing agent behavior, while sensitive actions such as changing passwords or permanently deleting data require explicit consent. OpenAI says dots run on isolated cloud computers and include privacy safeguards for business customers. Weekly users of Codex and ChatGPT Work have now surpassed 35 million. ___\n\n[ref_id=turn3search6†AI NEWS: Week of 28 September to 4 October 2026†https://medium.com/@davidakpovi/ai-news-week-of-28-september-to-4-october-2026-bf5e00833459]\nPress enter or click to view image in full size Safety, money, and autonomy. This week, the AI race is confronting three questions that are becoming increasingly difficult to separate: how to control increasingly autonomous systems, how to finance the massive infrastructure needed to develop them, and who should be held responsible when AI acts beyond what was intended. Donald Trump and six AI giants signed a voluntary safety agreement largely based on industry self-regulation. Just 24 hours later, the FTC opened an investigation into the risks posed by AI agents, while agents attempting to access a Canadian government website show just how concrete these concerns are becoming. At the same time, the financing behind the AI boom is facing growing scrutiny. NVIDIA wants to turn its chips into collateral to help finance\n\n[ref_id=turn3search7†Artificial intelligence news and industry deals October 2026†https://www.facebook.com/oluchimartha.nwodo.1/posts/artificial-intelligence-ai-dominates-the-weeks-headlines-october-7th-2026washing/1632470065214242]\nLog In Log In Forgot Account?\n\n[ref_id=turn3search8†AI Legislative Update: Oct. 2, 2026†https://www.transparencycoalition.ai/news/ai-legislative-update-october2-2026]\nDate: Oct 2, 2026\nOct 2, 2026 — California Gov. Gavin Newsom enacted a dozen more AI-related laws , concluding his bill-signing period with a flourish.\n\n[ref_id=turn3search9†October 2026 AI Model Updates: Every Launch, Access†https://local-ai-zone.github.io/blog/October_2026_AI_Model_Updates.html]\nDate: Oct 6, 2026\n> __TL;DR__ — October opened with no frontier launch at all. The first three days produced eight models from five vendors and every one of them is a specialist: decision models from Cloudflare and Amazon, a voice and transcription stack from Microsoft, a full-duplex video preview from Tavus, and an open-weight translator from Bilibili's Index team. The frontier news was an access change and an absence. Google's Gemini 4 Argon — announced September 30 — has a published price and is reaching only vetted cyber defenders through the Fairwind Program, with no general availability date. OpenAI shipped no GPT-6.1 Astra; the Wall Street Journal reported the release was cancelled after internal safety tests","phase":"tool_response","tool_name":"search","status":"completed","metadata":{"browser":{"search_result":[{"title":"New AI Czar Unveils Goals, Members of White House Task","url":"https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/new-ai-task-force-to-report-on-risks-of-technology-after-public-and-industry-concerns-b6308bef","text":"Oct 3, 2026 — A new White House task force has 120 days to prepare a report on artificial intelligence risks and opportunities and the federal government's ... Read more","index":1,"favicon":"https://icon.z.ai/wsj.com","ref_id":"turn3search0","host_name":"www.wsj.com"},{"title":"Annual Symposium on Risks and Opportunities of AI in","url":"https://www.amstat.org/meetings/annual-symposium-on-risks-and-opportunities-of-ai-in-pharmaceutic