to the same message, creating it if this run has not committed yet.\n- Use persist/ to store concise information that should carry over into subsequent runs of the same chat, such as persistent plot facts, unresolved threads, relationship states, and user style preferences.\n- **Do not** copy full chat history, final replies, tool results, or temporary reasoning into persist/.\n- Readable workspace directories: output/, scratch/, plan/, summaries/, persist/, tool-results/, skills/.\n- Writable workspace directories: output/, scratch/, plan/, summaries/, persist/.\n\u003e You may encounter: \"No visible workspace files found.\" This happens because there are no persisted files; please continue.\n# **Important**: Before calling workspace_finish, you **must successfully call workspace_commit at least once** so that the user can see the final chat message.\n# **Important**: Do not answer in plain text. Finish by calling workspace_finish.\n\n# Basic tool calling flow (adjusted based on the actual situation, but the flow must include workspace_commit + workspace_finish):\n\nA simple template you can follow:\n    (thoughts before actions)\n    (call tools)(optional)\n\n    Now I need to call \"workspace_commit\" once.\n    Good, it has been committed. Finally, don't forget to call \"workspace_finish\".\n\nYou also can follow commit-N-times template:\n    (thoughts before actions)\n    (workspace_read_file)\n    (worldinfo_read_activated)\n    (call workspace_commit with append mode)\n    (think)\n    (edit if necessary)\n    (workspace_commit with append mode)\n\nAnyway: TOOLS\u0026SKILLS IS ALL YOU NEED\n\nAvailable agents:\nNone.\n\n[Start a new Chat]\n\nMouha Vone: (that's not a test so i need you to focus with me) so i want to achieve a state of absolute peak human physical conditioning that emulates the concept of 'superhuman assassin', mainly inspired by characters like Corvo (Dishonored) Garou (one punch man) Ezio Auditore (assassin's creed), prince (prince of Persia)…etc\n\nAge: 19 years\nSex: Male\nHeight: 180 cm\nWeight: 67.3 kg\nShoulder Circumference: 115 cm (measured around the deltoids, outdated info. About 2 years since)\nWaist Circumference: 72 cm (measured at the natural waist, outdated)\nCurrent Shoulder-to-Waist Ratio: Approximately 1.597\n(Been almost 2 years since i measured. Outdated)\nTraining History: Approximately ~ 2.5 to 3 years or more of intermittent gym training, light cardio (max 45min jogging, very outdated) and light combat sports (such as occasional wrestling), it's very inconsistent.\nAll of the following info are outdated at least more than a year\nAbsolute Strength (Lower):\nPistol Squat (Left): 5 Reps.\nPistol Squat (Right): 5 Reps \nRelative Strength (Upper):\nPull-up Capacity: ~5-7 Reps (Neutral Grip).\nNeurological/Skill:\nProprioception (Balance):. Eyes-closed log stand \u003e 80 seconds.\nMobility: good mobility\nWork Capacity (GPP):\nAerobic Base: LOW.\nEndurance: HIGH. Proven ability to sustain low-intensity movement (walking) for 8-11 hours.\n\nSo these were some of my real data, analyse the data. My knowledge I've got good knowledge about training and how body works biologically (I've also studied things about supplements and gut in that rabbit hole…etc). i stopped training (it's hard to explain) as i didn't really find like something that help me archive my goals, i was motivated like no-one i was almost unstoppable it was like flames burning inside, then slowly it started dying, in my area there's almost zero too none mma or wrestling, even these if they were available doesn't quite target my goal, I've stopped the gym as it wasn't really building what am looking for. I've got a backyard, almost a garden, it got trees and stuff (you can ask if I've got some things, nothing professional tho) but i was honestly just too lazy to do stuff in it such as a pullup bar, i wanted to do also something like hanging a thick metal chain from somewhere high and i could use it to do stuff. I got depressed heavily. Tried to go back trying to re ignite that flame not too long ago about a month or two and failed as i went in deep depression, am still feeling depressed honestly and not into it but it's line am just kicking myself just to write that very message. Also it's not like i lack knowledge but like i found nothing that help me like compile it into something excitable, well that was before at least now i just feel lost and dead.\n\nFirst — you wrote this while feeling dead inside. That's not nothing. Doing the thing with the fire out is literally the core skill of this entire path. Remember what today took, because that's the same muscle everything else runs on.\n\nNow, the analysis you asked for.\n\n## What your data actually says\n\nRead as an athletic profile, you're **a mover with a missing engine.**\n\n- **180cm / 67.3kg** — light frame, high leverage. For your goal this is an *asset*, not something to fix. You don't need mass; you need strength-to-weight and bulletproof connective tissue.\n- **Balance \u003e80s eyes-closed** — genuinely rare proprioception. This is your natural talent seed. It's the raw material for rail work, precision jumps, acrobatics.\n- **Pistols 5/5** — solid single-leg base. Parkour lives on single-leg strength; every vault and landing is a pistol variant.\n- **Pull-ups 5–7** — this is your biggest gap. The archetype you want is *pull-dominant*: climbing, wall runs, muscle-ups, chain work. Those skills unlock around 10–12 strict pull-ups and get good past 15. Priority one.\n- **Aerobic base LOW, but 8–11h walking** — you don't lack endurance; you lack the middle layer (sustained zone-2 output). This is the *fastest-fixing* quality in your whole profile. It responds in weeks.\n- Your \"Endurance: HIGH\" entry is really a measurement of willpower and pain tolerance. Keep it. It's your most transferable asset.\n\n**Verdict:** balanced, light, pain-tolerant, mobile — weak pull, no engine. Which is, coincidentally, the exact profile of someone who becomes *very* good at parkour and climbing once the pull and the engine are built.\n\nOne note: your data is over a year stale, so assume detrained. Fine — muscle memory returns fast. Expect most of your old numbers back within 4–8 weeks. Don't retest maxes in week one.\n\n## Why the flame died\n\nThis matters more than the program. You didn't lose motivation because you're weak — you lost it because **the goal had no executable form.** \"Superhuman assassin\" is an image, and images burn on novelty. There was nothing in your environment shaped like the fantasy, so the fire had nothing to grip, and it burned out. That's not a character flaw, it's mechanics.\n\nAlso: that unbuilt pull-up bar has been taxing you for two years. Every time you see the backyard, it's a monument to the version of you who didn't finish. That's expensive. Building it isn't just equipment — it closes the loop and flips the space from a reminder into an invitation.\n\nHere's the key move: **what you're describing has a real name.** It's a *hybrid movement athlete* — parkour + climbing + combat conditioning. Ezio's movement is just parkour with a costume budget. Once the fantasy has a name, it has training methods, progressions, and metrics. Fantasy became compile-able the moment it got a name.\n\n## Step 1 — Build the ground (one afternoon, ~$80–100)\n\n- **A thick tree branch is your pull-up bar.** Bonus: thick bar = free grip work. If no branch is right, a wall-mounted bar is cheap.\n- **The chain — your instinct is correct.** 6–10m of thick-link chain over a tested limb. Climb it hand-over-hand, do pull-ups on it, one-arm hangs. This is your villa wall. Safety: pad underneath, start with legs assisting, tape grip zones — grip strength fails *suddenly* with zero warning, so stay low until it's proven.\n- **Gymnastic rings** (~$25) from the same branch — rows, dips, muscle-up path, front lever later. Highest ROI object in existence.\n- **Sandbag** — duffel bag + play sand. Carries, ground-to-shoulder, squats. The assassin's implement.\n- **Jump rope**, and a backpack for rucking (zone 2 while loaded — very Corvo).\n\n## Step 2 — The on-ramp (weeks 1–4, depression-proof)\n\nDaily minimum: **15 minutes, dirt-floor small.** Example: 3 dead hangs, 10 squats, 5 minutes walking, one skill snack (balance on a curb, crawl patterns). That's it. That's a passing day.\n\nRules:\n- A 5-minute session counts.\n- **Never miss twice.**\n- You may do more only when it *pulls* you — never as an obligation.\n\nThe floor exists because the dead days decide the whole project. The ignite-then-crash cycle you described is a classic depression pattern, not a discipline failure — so the entry point has to be small enough to survive your worst day.\n\n## Step 3 — The template (week 5 onward)\n\n| Day | Session |\n|---|---|\n| Mon | **Pull strength** — pull-up ladders (5 sets of ~half your max, add reps weekly), ring rows, push-up/dip progression, pistol progression, hollow holds |\n| Tue | **Engine** — 20 min easy run, nasal breathing only. +5 min/week toward 45 |\n| Wed | **Climb \u0026 carry** — dead hangs, chain climbs (legs → no legs), sandbag shoulder + farmer carries, hanging knee raises |\n| Thu | **Engine easy + skill** — walk/run, then 10 min: rail balance, bear/crab/ape crawling (yes, that's Garou-coded), vault reps over a low wall |\n| Fri | **Strength B** — variation: strict pull-ups, dips, single-leg work, rotational power (sandbag or sledgehammer) |\n| Sat | **Ezio day** — 60–90 min ruck + play: climb, vault, practice rolls. Joy is the point. |\n| Sun | Off / 10 min mobility |\n\nFrom month ~4: add one sprint day (hill strides), broad jumps → precision landings, muscle-up/one-arm pull progressions, and structured beginner parkour progressions (safety roll first — it's the most assassin skill and the most protective one you'll ever learn).\n\n**The scoreboard — retest every 8 weeks, log everything:**\n1. Max dead hang (time)\n2. Strict pull-ups (max)\n3. Pistols per side\n4. Broad jump (best of 3)\n5. 2km time trial\n6. Eyes-closed balance test (your old test — continuity matters)\n7. Chain climb height, no legs\n\nThis is the \"compilation\" you said you never found: the fantasy converted into seven numbers that only move if you do. For a data-brained person, this is how motivation survives mood.\n\n## Two honest limits\n\n**Fighting cannot be solo'd.** Skill needs partners and coaching — that's non-negotiable. What you *can* build alone is the entire chassis: breakfalls, technical stand-ups, sprawls, ground flow, shadow work, rotational power. At 19, geography almost certainly changes within a few years (study, work, city). You build the machine now; you walk into a real gym later already pre-built. Also — double-check whether \"no MMA near me\" actually means \"none within walking distance.\" Any boxing, judo, or wrestling a bus ride away is worth a bus ride.\n\n**Ezio drops are fiction; your knees are real.** Progress landings from ankle height, learn rolls before jumps, test every anchor.\n\nAnd the reframe you need: in 12–24 months this scoreboard reads 15–20 pull-ups, unassisted chain climb, vaulting rails at speed, 10km comfortable, walking into any climbing gym and holding your own. That's top-percentile human at moving through the world — the fantasy, minus the cape. \"Superhuman\" is a *direction*, and the scoreboard is how you walk it.\n\nOne last thing, and I'll keep it short because you know your own head: exercise is one of the best-evidenced tools against depression that exists — but it works as a **daily dose, not a heroic dose**. That's what the floor is for. If the heaviness deepens instead of lifting as you move, a doctor or therapist isn't a detour from this path — it's health-bar maintenance. Garou still sleeps and eats.\n\nThree questions so I can turn the template into a full detailed program with sets, progressions, and standards:\n\n1. What's actually in the backyard — branch height/