vement athlete* — parkour + climbing + combat conditioning. Ezio's movement is just parkour with a costume budget. Once the fantasy has a name, it has training methods, progressions, and metrics. Fantasy became compile-able the moment it got a name.\n\n## Step 1 — Build the ground (one afternoon, ~$80–100)\n\n- **A thick tree branch is your pull-up bar.** Bonus: thick bar = free grip work. If no branch is right, a wall-mounted bar is cheap.\n- **The chain — your instinct is correct.** 6–10m of thick-link chain over a tested limb. Climb it hand-over-hand, do pull-ups on it, one-arm hangs. This is your villa wall. Safety: pad underneath, start with legs assisting, tape grip zones — grip strength fails *suddenly* with zero warning, so stay low until it's proven.\n- **Gymnastic rings** (~$25) from the same branch — rows, dips, muscle-up path, front lever later. Highest ROI object in existence.\n- **Sandbag** — duffel bag + play sand. Carries, ground-to-shoulder, squats. The assassin's implement.\n- **Jump rope**, and a backpack for rucking (zone 2 while loaded — very Corvo).\n\n## Step 2 — The on-ramp (weeks 1–4, depression-proof)\n\nDaily minimum: **15 minutes, dirt-floor small.** Example: 3 dead hangs, 10 squats, 5 minutes walking, one skill snack (balance on a curb, crawl patterns). That's it. That's a passing day.\n\nRules:\n- A 5-minute session counts.\n- **Never miss twice.**\n- You may do more only when it *pulls* you — never as an obligation.\n\nThe floor exists because the dead days decide the whole project. The ignite-then-crash cycle you described is a classic depression pattern, not a discipline failure — so the entry point has to be small enough to survive your worst day.\n\n## Step 3 — The template (week 5 onward)\n\n| Day | Session |\n|---|---|\n| Mon | **Pull strength** — pull-up ladders (5 sets of ~half your max, add reps weekly), ring rows, push-up/dip progression, pistol progression, hollow holds |\n| Tue | **Engine** — 20 min easy run, nasal breathing only. +5 min/week toward 45 |\n| Wed | **Climb & carry** — dead hangs, chain climbs (legs → no legs), sandbag shoulder + farmer carries, hanging knee raises |\n| Thu | **Engine easy + skill** — walk/run, then 10 min: rail balance, bear/crab/ape crawling (yes, that's Garou-coded), vault reps over a low wall |\n| Fri | **Strength B** — variation: strict pull-ups, dips, single-leg work, rotational power (sandbag or sledgehammer) |\n| Sat | **Ezio day** — 60–90 min ruck + play: climb, vault, practice rolls. Joy is the point. |\n| Sun | Off / 10 min mobility |\n\nFrom month ~4: add one sprint day (hill strides), broad jumps → precision landings, muscle-up/one-arm pull progressions, and structured beginner parkour progressions (safety roll first — it's the most assassin skill and the most protective one you'll ever learn).\n\n**The scoreboard — retest every 8 weeks, log everything:**\n1. Max dead hang (time)\n2. Strict pull-ups (max)\n3. Pistols per side\n4. Broad jump (best of 3)\n5. 2km time trial\n6. Eyes-closed balance test (your old test — continuity matters)\n7. Chain climb height, no legs\n\nThis is the \"compilation\" you said you never found: the fantasy converted into seven numbers that only move if you do. For a data-brained person, this is how motivation survives mood.\n\n## Two honest limits\n\n**Fighting cannot be solo'd.** Skill needs partners and coaching — that's non-negotiable. What you *can* build alone is the entire chassis: breakfalls, technical stand-ups, sprawls, ground flow, shadow work, rotational power. At 19, geography almost certainly changes within a few years (study, work, city). You build the machine now; you walk into a real gym later already pre-built. Also — double-check whether \"no MMA near me\" actually means \"none within walking distance.\" Any boxing, judo, or wrestling a bus ride away is worth a bus ride.\n\n**Ezio drops are fiction; your knees are real.** Progress landings from ankle height, learn rolls before jumps, test every anchor.\n\nAnd the reframe you need: in 12–24 months this scoreboard reads 15–20 pull-ups, unassisted chain climb, vaulting rails at speed, 10km comfortable, walking into any climbing gym and holding your own. That's top-percentile human at moving through the world — the fantasy, minus the cape. \"Superhuman\" is a *direction*, and the scoreboard is how you walk it.\n\nOne last thing, and I'll keep it short because you know your own head: exercise is one of the best-evidenced tools against depression that exists — but it works as a **daily dose, not a heroic dose**. That's what the floor is for. If the heaviness deepens instead of lifting as you move, a doctor or therapist isn't a detour from this path — it's health-bar maintenance. Garou still sleeps and eats.\n\nThree questions so I can turn the template into a full detailed program with sets, progressions, and standards:\n\n1. What's actually in the backyard — branch height/thickness, any wall, rail, or ledge nearby?\n2. Rough budget for equipment?\n3. Anything within an hour or two's travel — climbing gym, parkour spot, any combat sports club at all?\n\nYou kicked yourself to write one message today, Mouha. That's the whole skill. Everything after this is just reps.\n\nOk so let's take things step by step and not get too hasty and starting to jump to generate workout plans but instead they should be carefully measured and intelegntly set and crafted for the best (don't construct any plan untill i tell you focus on the other things). So first i don't want like traditional training or like generic stuff, my ambitions are above the stars so conventional training won't do it. So first we need you to assess me so you have concrete data you can build on. I've found these two videos that i think are extremely necessary for reaching my goal: https://youtu.be/2-7QZVEjW9U?si=8n_kUWZrQt7TBgO0\nhttps://youtu.be/70i14YxUTbc?si=v2gXWvhVCDX4XGR6 \nThey explain a approach that makes sense and actually feel like it directly feed my ambitions (more details in what the vids explain ofc) \nAlso i think one of the most necessary things are for discipline and never stopping is missions or something a bit \"gamefied\". Also I want to be able to kill people according to superhuman assassin goal and know how to use stuff not only boxing or grappling.\nAnd for your questions:\nSo in my garden there's a lot of things more than i count, there's a lot of things that can be used in different ways there are tree branches, thick bar (i cant close my hand around it), heavy metal bar that i usually used to swing (like mace exercises) it or throw it like a spear, I've got rubber bands different sizes, I've got walls big and small also walls with holes in them, I've got a chain... etc there's things that i may have forgot to mention but pretty much there's a lot of freedom. 2 my budget isn't really high but i can try to manage 3 sadly no, i just faintly remeber that there's a small parkour club and there's also mma and wrestling but it's in a different state that's almost 2 hours away and they usually train at night and i can't go home in time (also family issues).\n\nLet me identify those two videos first so I can give you a real analysis of the methodology instead of guessing.Both videos identified. Let me dig into the Mover's Odyssey methodology and the tendon training system so I can give you a real analysis, not a guess.Found the source. Both videos are from **Mover's Odyssey** — video 1 is the tendon training philosophy, video 2 is the maximal-effort isometric method. Let me pull the actual methodology details so the analysis is grounded in what the videos actually teach.The ref IDs got crossed between searches. Let me open the Mover's Odyssey pages directly by URL, and search for the gamification angle in parallel.The article cut off at the methods section — that's the part I need most. Let me get the rest.The methodology is clear now. Two more pieces to grab — the end of the article, and the \"Weapons of Flow\" program details since weapons are core to your goal.Both videos are from the same source — **Mover's Odyssey** — and together they describe one coherent system: a tendon-first training philosophy borrowed from 1500 years of Kung Fu, updated with modern isometric science. Your instinct on these was right, and it's worth understanding *why* before we touch anything else, because this methodology is almost perfectly matched to your archetype and your body type.\n\n## What the videos actually teach\n\n**Video 1 — \"Strengthening Tendons Instead of Muscles\"** makes the case that conventional strength training trains the wrong tissue. Tendons are dense collagen springs: they store kinetic energy and release it explosively, they absorb the 3–4x bodyweight landing forces that muscles can't, and they produce movement for nearly zero metabolic cost. Kung Fu calls tendon training \"longevity training\" — when you're old and muscles become expensive to fuel, the tendons still work at full strength.【turn1fetch0】\n\nThe analogy from the article that you should tattoo somewhere: **wolves and bears both hit 40 mph, but the bear burns out in seconds on raw muscle while the wolf cruises for miles on elastic tendons.**【turn1fetch0】 Your 67kg frame was never the bear build. It's the wolf build. You were born with the right chassis for this method — that's part of why the gym always felt wrong and why these videos felt right.\n\n**Video 2 — the 57% study video** — supplies the modern tool: maximal-effort isometric training. Pushing or pulling against an immovable object recruits your largest motor units almost immediately, rebuilds tendon tissue, and primes the nervous system so everything else you do gets stronger.【turn0search5】 The companion concept, *yielding isometrics* — holding a position as load tries to collapse it — is the \"ancient strength method that fixes modern bodies\" version of the same idea.【turn0search5���\n\nThe full system uses three methods, each giving the tendon a different adaptation:【turn1fetch0】\n\n1. **Isometrics** — muscles tire, load shifts to the tendons, tendons thicken. This is the bulletproofing layer. The Kung Fu horse stance is the classic example; advanced practitioners hold it for 30 minutes.\n2. **Repetitive eccentrics** — lowering under load. This is why climbers have visibly thick wrist flexor tendons while bodybuilders with three times the arm muscle have tendons you can't even see. The force-absorption layer.\n3. **Plyometrics** — rebound work, the spring layer. Highest injury risk, which is why it comes last, only on top of a built base.\n\nNotice what this means for you: your light frame, your pull-up weakness, your missing engine — none of these are problems *within this system*. A light body with strong tendons is the highest power-to-weight machine a human can build. This is the sinewy-climber, silent-mover body, not the gym body. The method you found isn't just non-conventional; it's the tissue-level foundation of the exact archetype you're chasing — springy, efficient, injury-proof, near-silent in movement. Corvo, Ezio, the Prince: every one of them is a tendon-dominant mover.\n\n## The missions layer\n\nThis is the part you asked about that most training advice gets completely wrong, and the research is genuinely on your side. The concept is called **RPG fitness**: training wrapped inside a role-playing game where the story advances one day at a time and each chapter is paired to a real workout. The mechanism that makes it work isn't discipline — it's curiosity. Discipline dies on your dead days. Curiosity survives them, because you want to know what happens next.【turn0search3】\n\nThe behavioral science backs this: gamified exercise produces a measurable increase in physical activity across trials, and playing a capable character genuinely shifts how you see yourself.【turn0search3】 For someone fighting depression, that second effect is the one that matters. The missions aren't a motivational trick — they're identity scaffolding.\n\nYour world is already built for this. The garden is the map. Missions are scenarios that happen to contain the training inside them: \"infiltrate the compound\" is a climbing route up the walls with holes; \"the courier\" is a loaded carry between landmarks; \"survive the interrogation\" is holding isometric positions under accumulating fatigue; \"escape the rooftops\" is a vault-and-precision circuit. The story supplies the reason, the reason carries you through the session, and the session quietly builds the assassin. When we eventually get to programming, we build the mission system with the training hidden inside it — not the other way around.\n\n## The weapons dimension\n\nHonest framing, since you asked for it straight: the \"know how to use stuff\" part has a real path, and it runs through the same system you found. Mover's Odyssey's own programs include **Weapons of Flow — mastery of ancient Indian clubs and maces**【turn1fetch1】, and you already own the tool: that heavy metal bar you swing and throw. Mace work builds rotational power, crushing grip, shoulder durability, and whole-body coordination — the exact attribute set underneath every weapons-using fighter. Spear throwing is power plus precision. The chain is climbing weaponized. The hole-riddled walls are your terrain puzzle.\n\nThe part that stays fictional for now: live fighting skill with weapons requires partners and coaching — kali, HEMA, fencing, whatever becomes accessible when your life expands (and at 19, it will expand). No solo system replaces that. But everything *underneath* it — tendon durability, rotational snap, grip that doesn't fail, flow between positions — is fully buildable alone in that garden, starting now. You build the machine; the skill bolts on later. Knowing that structure now saves you the crash of expecting the wrong thing from solo training.\n\n## The assessment — the concrete data step\n\nThis is not a workout plan. It's calibration. Your numbers are over a year stale, which means anything built now is built blind. Perform these over three days, rested, in this order, and report the numbers back. That dataset becomes the foundation everything gets constructed on.\n\n**Day 1 — strength & tendon (fully rested):**\n\n| Test | Protocol | What it tells us |\n|---|---|---|\n| Max dead hang | Thick branch, full grip, hang until failure. Time it. | Tendon + grip capacity — the single most important number for your archetype |\n| Max pull-ups | Any grip, dead hang each rep, no kipping. | Relative pulling strength |\n| Horse stance hold | Wide low squat, thighs parallel, arms extended front. Time to failure. | The Kung Fu tendon test from the video — direct lower-body tendon benchmark |\n| Max push-ups | Full range, controlled, stop at form failure. | Pressing base |\n\n**Day 2 — power, balance, mobility:**\n\n| Test | Protocol | What it tells us |\n|---|---|---|\n| Standing broad jump | Three attempts, measure the best. | Explosive power — the tendon spring in action |\n| Pistols per side | Full controlled range. Compare to your old 5/5. | Single-leg strength continuity |\n| Eyes-closed single-leg stand | Same conditions as your old 80s test. | Proprioception continuity |\n| Overhead deep squat | Arms raised overhead, squat to full depth. Note: heels down? arms vertical? any collapse? | Mobility baseline — the plyometrics gate |\n\n**Day 3 — engine & recovery:**\n\n| Test | Protocol | What it tells us |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2km time trial | Run at 80% effort, not a race. Time it. | The aerobic gap, quantified |\n| Morning resting heart rate | Before rising, three consecutive mornings, average. | Recovery / autonomic baseline |\n\nTen numbers. When you report them back, we'll have the real map — which tendon qualities are sleeping, how big the engine gap actually is, and what the first phase of any real programming needs to target. The videos gave us the method. The missions will give us the fuel. The assessment gives us ground truth.\n\nTake the three days when you can. The numbers don't need to be good — they need to be *real*.\n\n[system reminder, direct output recovery attempt 1] You replied with plain text, but this run must continue through Agent tools until workspace_finish. Inspect the workspace if needed, produce the answer through workspace_write_file and workspace_commit, then call workspace_finish. I saved your direct text to output/direct_output.md. If that text is the intended reply, call workspace_commit with path \"output/direct_output.md\" before workspace_finish. Do NOT answer directly in plain text.\n\nMy previous reply was saved as the direct output file. Let me verify its contents before committing it to the chat.<tool_call>tool_call: workspace_read_file]({\"path\": \"output/direct_output.md\", \"line_count\": 200})\n\n[system reminder, direct output recovery attempt 2] You replied with plain text, but this run must continue through Agent tools until workspace_finish. Inspect the workspace if needed, produce the answer through workspace_write_file and workspace_commit, then call workspace_finish. I saved your direct text to output/direct_output.md. If that text is the intended reply, call workspace_commit with path \"output/direct_output.md\" before workspace_finish. Do NOT answer directly in plain text.\n\nI need to verify the saved direct output file before committing it to the chat.\n{\"name\":\"workspace_read_file\",\"arguments\":{\"line_count\":200,\"path\":\"output/direct_output.md\"}}\n\n\n[Tool result for call_5840de7c_0]: workspace_read_file: output/direct_output.md lines 1-1 of 1, chars 213 of 213, words 36 of 36\n1 | My previous reply was saved as the direct output file. Let me verify its contents before committing it to the chat.<tool_call>tool_call: workspace_read_file]({\"path\": \"output/direct_output.md\", \"line_count\": 200})\n\nThe saved file contains only my preamble text (36 words), not the actual reply — the full c